In modern MSTest (v3+), you can inject `TestContext` via the constructor and use `[ClassInitialize]` / `[TestInitialize]` for sync setup, plus their async lifecycle equivalents. Here's the recommended pattern:

```csharp
[TestClass]
public class MyTests
{
    // Constructor injection for TestContext (MSTest v3+)
    public TestContext TestContext { get; }

    public MyTests(TestContext testContext)
    {
        TestContext = testContext;
        // Synchronous per-test setup (runs before each test)
    }

    // One-time async class-level setup
    [ClassInitialize]
    public static async Task ClassInit(TestContext context)
    {
        // e.g., spin up a shared resource, seed a database
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // Per-test async setup (runs before each test, after constructor)
    [TestInitialize]
    public async Task TestInit()
    {
        TestContext.WriteLine("Setting up test...");
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public void SyncTest()
    {
        Assert.IsNotNull(TestContext);
    }

    [TestMethod]
    public async Task AsyncTest()
    {
        await Task.Delay(10);
        Assert.IsNotNull(TestContext);
    }

    // Per-test async teardown
    [TestCleanup]
    public async Task TestCleanup()
    {
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }

    // One-time async class-level teardown
    [ClassCleanup]
    public static async Task ClassTeardown()
    {
        await Task.CompletedTask;
    }
}
```

**Key points:**

- **Constructor injection** of `TestContext` is the modern MSTest v3+ approach (replaces the old `public TestContext TestContext { get; set; }` property setter pattern).
- The **execution order** per test is: `ClassInitialize` → constructor → `TestInitialize` → test method → `TestCleanup`.
- `[ClassInitialize]`/`[ClassCleanup]` can be `async Task`.
- Put **sync-only** setup in the constructor; use `[TestInitialize]` for anything `async`.
